module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.Immersion
public import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.KoszulRegularSequence
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.KoszulRegularImmersionNear
import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.IdealSheaf.Basic
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.Ideal
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.Localization
import WeilConjectures.AlgebraicGeometry.KoszulComplex
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.KoszulRegularFlatBaseChange

/-! # Composition of Koszul-regular immersions

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 067Q, assertion (2), with the definition
in Tag 063J and the concatenation lemma in More on Algebra, Tag 0669
(blueprint: lem-sfb-koszul-immersion-composition).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Koszul-regular immersions are stable under composition. The immersion
instance of the composite is `IsImmersion.comp`; the conclusion records its
Koszul-regularity at every source point, using the existing local predicate.
Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 067Q (2), with Tag 063J
(blueprint: lem-sfb-koszul-immersion-composition).

Proof:
1. By `lem-weil-pbc-proper-fp-permanence` (e), the composite is an immersion.
2. Fix a source point. Factor both immersions through closed immersions into
open targets. Remove from the ambient open the closed complement of the chosen
open in the intermediate closed subscheme, so the two closed charts agree.
Use `lem-sfb-koszul-closed-immersion-affine-criterion` to obtain an affine
chart Spec A of the target, with the intermediate chart Spec B, where B = A/I
and I has finite Koszul-regular generators f. The chart ideals are those of
`def-weil-closed-immersion` and `def-sbc-closed-immersion-ideal-sheaf`.
3. The principal clause of that affine criterion gives a principal open of
Spec B on which the first immersion has Koszul-regular generators g-bar.
Lift its defining element to A and localize. By
`lem-weil-pbc-localization-quotient-commute` the quotient commutes with this
localization. By `lem-weil-pbc-localization-flat` and
`lem-sc-koszul-regular-flat-base-change`, f remains Koszul-regular there.
Lift the generators g-bar to elements g of this localized A.
4. The kernel of the composite quotient is generated by (f,g): lift the
coefficients of an expression modulo I and the remaining difference lies in I.
The reverse inclusion follows because all these generators map to zero.
5. In `def-weil-pbc-koszul-complex`, degree-zero homology is the quotient by
the generators. Together with `def-weil-pbc-koszul-regular-sequence`, this
makes the quotient augmentations K(f) to B and K_B(g-bar) to B/(g-bar)
quasi-isomorphisms. Reindex them into the nonpositive cohomological convention.
6. The concatenation-resolution clause of `lem-sfb-koszul-evaluation-pasting`
identifies the augmented total Koszul complex with a resolution of B/(g-bar).
Thus (f,g) is Koszul-regular, by the same definition of Koszul regularity.
7. Principal localization supplies such charts inside every prescribed open
neighbourhood; flat base change preserves the generators' Koszul regularity,
and localization commutes with the quotient. The reverse direction of
`lem-sfb-koszul-closed-immersion-affine-criterion` yields the local ideal
condition for the composite. The point was arbitrary. The empty source is
vacuous, and either generator tuple may be empty, since its Koszul complex
is the ring concentrated in degree zero. -/
theorem isKoszulRegularImmersionNear_comp {Z Y X : Scheme.{u}}
    (i : Z ⟶ Y) (j : Y ⟶ X) [IsImmersion i] [IsImmersion j]
    (hi : ∀ z : Z, IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear i z)
    (hj : ∀ y : Y, IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear j y) :
    ∀ z : Z, IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear (i ≫ j) z := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
